**Sec. 70401.** This section increases the employer-provided child care credit by raising the percentage of qualified expenditures taken into account from 25% to 40% (50% for eligible small businesses) and increasing the maximum credit limit to $500,000 ($600,000 for eligible small businesses), with annual inflation adjustments after 2026. It defines an "eligible small business" as one meeting a 5-taxable-year gross receipts test and allows credits for child care provided under contracts with intermediate entities. The provision also clarifies that a facility remains qualified if jointly owned or operated by the taxpayer and others, and requires the Treasury Secretary to issue necessary regulations. These amendments apply to amounts paid or incurred after December 31, 2025.

**Sec. 70402.** This section makes the adoption credit partially refundable by treating the portion of the credit not exceeding $5,000 as a refundable credit under subpart C. It establishes annual inflation adjustments for the credit amounts, with the refundable portion indexed to the cost-of-living adjustment starting in 2025. The refundable portion is excluded from the carryforward rules for non-refundable credits. These changes apply to taxable years beginning after December 31, 2024.

**Sec. 70403.** This section amends the definition of "special needs" for the adoption credit to include determinations made by Indian tribal governments, in addition to states. This allows adoptions facilitated by tribal governments to qualify for the enhanced credit for special needs children. The amendment applies to taxable years beginning after December 31, 2024.

**Sec. 70404.** This section increases the annual exclusion for employer-provided dependent care assistance from $5,000 to $7,500 (or $3,750 for married individuals filing separately). This allows employees to exclude a higher amount of employer-paid dependent care expenses from their gross income. The amendment applies to taxable years beginning after December 31, 2025.

**Sec. 70405.** This section enhances the child and dependent care tax credit by raising the maximum applicable percentage from 35% to 50%. The credit phases down by 1 percentage point for every $2,000 of adjusted gross income over $15,000, but not below 35%, and further phases down by 1 percentage point for every $2,000 ($4,000 for joint filers) over $75,000 ($150,000 for joint filers), but not below 20%. These changes apply to taxable years beginning after December 31, 2025.

**Sec. 70411.** This section creates a new tax credit (Section 25F) for individuals who make qualified contributions to scholarship granting organizations in participating states, capped at $1,700 per taxpayer per year. It also excludes scholarship amounts received by eligible students (household income ≤ 300% of area median income) for qualified elementary and secondary education expenses from gross income under new Section 139K. Scholarship granting organizations must be 501(c)(3) entities, spend at least 90% of income on scholarships, and provide scholarships to at least 10 students not all attending the same school. The credit and income exclusion apply to taxable years ending after December 31, 2026.

**Sec. 70412.** This section makes the exclusion for employer payments of student loans permanent by removing the sunset provision that limited it to payments made before January 1, 2026. It adds an annual inflation adjustment to the $5,250 exclusion limit for taxable years beginning after 2026. The amendment applies to payments made after December 31, 2025.

**Sec. 70413.** This section expands the definition of "qualified higher education expenses" for 529 accounts to include tuition, books, online materials, tutoring, standardized test fees, dual enrollment fees, and educational therapies for students in elementary or secondary public, private, or religious schools. It also doubles the annual limit on tax-free distributions from 529 accounts for K-12 tuition from $10,000 to $20,000. The expansion of qualified expenses applies to distributions made after the date of enactment, while the increased limit applies to taxable years beginning after December 31, 2025.

**Sec. 70414.** This section adds "qualified postsecondary credentialing expenses" to the definition of qualified higher education expenses for 529 accounts, covering tuition, fees, books, and testing costs for recognized postsecondary credential programs. Recognized programs include those on state lists under the Workforce Innovation and Opportunity Act, in the WEAMS directory, or identified by the Treasury Secretary. Recognized credentials include industry-recognized certificates, apprenticeship completions, and occupational licenses. The amendment applies to distributions made after the date of enactment.

**Sec. 70415.** This section rewrites the excise tax on investment income of private colleges and universities (Section 4968) to impose a tiered tax of 1.4%, 4%, or 8% based on student adjusted endowment levels of $500,000, $750,000, and $2,000,000, respectively. It defines "applicable educational institution" as having at least 3,000 tuition-paying students, with more than 50% located in the U.S., and a student adjusted endowment of at least $500,000. The tax base includes net investment income, with specific rules to include student loan interest and federally-subsidized royalty income. The amendments apply to taxable years beginning after December 31, 2025.

**Sec. 70416.** This section expands the definition of "covered employee" for the excise tax on excess compensation in tax-exempt organizations to include any current or former employee who was employed during any taxable year beginning after December 31, 2016. This broadens the scope of employees subject to the 1.4% excise tax on excess compensation and the 20% excise tax on excess parachute payments. The amendment applies to taxable years beginning after December 31, 2025.

**Sec. 70421.** This section makes Opportunity Zones permanent by establishing decennial designations starting July 1, 2026, and repealing the special rule for Puerto Rico. It modifies the definition of "low-income community" to include census tracts with median family income ≤ 70% of the state/metro median or poverty rate ≥ 20% and median income ≤ 125% of the state/metro median. It extends the capital gains deferral period to 5 years (with a 10% basis step-up) and allows a 100% basis step-up for investments held 10 years, with special 30% basis step-up for rural opportunity funds. It also imposes new information reporting requirements on qualified opportunity funds and businesses, with penalties for non-compliance.

**Sec. 70422.** This section permanently increases the state housing credit ceiling for the Low-Income Housing Tax Credit (LIHTC) by setting the multiplier at 1.12 for calendar years after 2025. It modifies the tax-exempt bond financing requirement, allowing the credit to be claimed if 50% or more of the building's basis is financed with tax-exempt bonds, or if 25% or more is financed with bonds issued after December 31, 2025, and at least 5% of the basis is financed by such bonds. The amendments apply to calendar years beginning after December 31, 2025, and buildings placed in service in taxable years beginning after that date.

**Sec. 70423.** This section permanently extends the New Markets Tax Credit (NMTC) by removing the sunset provision that limited it to calendar years 2020 through 2025. It allows unused annual limitations to be carried forward for up to five calendar years, with pre-2026 excesses treated as occurring in 2025. The amendments apply to calendar years beginning after December 31, 2025.

**Sec. 70424.** This section permanently reinstates and expands the partial deduction for charitable contributions made by individuals who do not itemize, increasing the limit from $300 ($600 for joint filers) to $1,000 ($2,000 for joint filers). It removes the sunset provision that limited the deduction to taxable years beginning in 2021. The amendments apply to taxable years beginning after December 31, 2025.

**Sec. 70425.** This section imposes a 0.5% floor on the deduction for charitable contributions made by individuals, meaning contributions are deductible only to the extent they exceed 0.5% of the taxpayer's contribution base. It modifies the limitation on cash contributions to 60% of the contribution base and adjusts carryforward rules to account for contributions disallowed by the new floor. The amendments apply to taxable years beginning after December 31, 2025.

**Sec. 70426.** This section imposes a 1% floor on the deduction for charitable contributions made by corporations, meaning contributions are deductible only to the extent they exceed 1% of taxable income but do not exceed 10% of taxable income. It modifies carryforward rules to allow contributions disallowed by the 1% floor to be carried forward only from years in which the 10% limitation is exceeded, and limits carryforwards to five years. The amendments apply to taxable years beginning after December 31, 2025.

**Sec. 70427.** This section permanently increases the limitation on the cover-over of the tax on distilled spirits from $13.00 to $13.25 per proof gallon. This affects the amount of federal excise tax that can be covered over by state taxes on imported distilled spirits. The amendment applies to distilled spirits brought into the United States after December 31, 2025.

**Sec. 70428.** This section treats activities substantially related to participation or investment in fisheries in the Bering Sea and Aleutian Islands as substantially related to the exempt purpose of certain nonprofit entities identified in the Magnuson-Stevens Fishery Conservation and Management Act. It allows these entities to transfer assets from wholly owned subsidiaries engaged in such activities without recognizing gain or income, provided the transfer occurs within 18 months of enactment. The section remains effective during the existence of the western Alaska community development quota program.

**Sec. 70429.** This section increases the limit on the charitable deduction for certain expenses incurred in support of Native Alaskan subsistence whaling from $10,000 to $50,000. This allows donors to deduct a higher amount of contributions supporting this specific activity. The amendment applies to taxable years beginning after December 31, 2025.

**Sec. 70430.** This section expands the exception to the percentage of completion method of accounting to include "residential construction contracts" in addition to "home construction contracts." It extends the contract duration requirement from 2 years to 3 years for non-home residential construction contracts. The amendment applies to contracts entered into in taxable years beginning after the date of enactment.

**Sec. 70431.** This section expands the qualified small business stock (QSBS) gain exclusion by increasing the exclusion percentage to 50% for stock held 3 years, 75% for 4 years, and 100% for 5 years or more, for stock acquired after the enactment date. It increases the per-issuer limitation from $10 million to $15 million for stock acquired after the enactment date, with inflation adjustments, and raises the aggregate gross assets limit from $50 million to $75 million. The amendments apply to taxable years beginning after the date of enactment, with specific rules for stock acquired before and after the applicable date.

**Sec. 70432.** This section repeals the revision to de minimis rules for third-party network transactions made by the American Rescue Plan Act of 2021, reinstating the requirement that third-party settlement organizations report only if transactions exceed $20,000 and 200 transactions. It applies the de minimis rule to backup withholding, requiring reporting only if aggregate transactions exceed the thresholds, unless prior year transactions were reportable. The reinstatement takes effect as if included in the American Rescue Plan Act, while the backup withholding changes apply to calendar years beginning after December 31, 2024.

**Sec. 70433.** This section increases the threshold for requiring information reporting under Section 6041 from $600 to $2,000, with annual inflation adjustments after 2026. It applies this new threshold to reporting on remuneration for services and backup withholding. The amendments apply to payments made after December 31, 2025.

**Sec. 70434.** This section allows taxpayers to elect to expense costs for qualified sound recording productions, capped at $150,000 per production or in aggregate per year. It defines a qualified sound recording production as a sound recording produced and recorded in the United States. It also allows bonus depreciation for qualified sound recording productions, treating them as placed in service at the time of initial release or broadcast. The amendments apply to productions commencing in taxable years ending after the date of enactment.

**Sec. 70435.** This section excludes 25% of the interest received by qualified lenders on qualified real estate loans secured by rural or agricultural real property from gross income. Qualified lenders include banks, savings associations, insurance companies, and certain federally chartered instrumentalities. Qualified real estate loans are those secured by rural or agricultural real estate, leasehold mortgages on such property, or aquaculture facilities, and made to persons other than specified foreign entities. The amendment applies to taxable years ending after the date of enactment.

**Sec. 70436.** This section reduces the transfer and manufacturing taxes on firearms to $0 for all firearms except machineguns and destructive devices, which remain taxed at $200 per unit. It amends Sections 5811 and 5821 to reflect these new rates. The amendments apply to calendar quarters beginning more than 90 days after the date of enactment.

**Sec. 70437.** This section allows taxpayers to elect to pay the net income tax attributable to gain from the sale or exchange of qualified farmland property to a qualified farmer in four equal annual installments. Qualified farmland property must have been used for farming purposes for substantially all of the preceding 10 years and be subject to a covenant prohibiting non-farming use for 10 years after the sale. The election must be made by the due date of the return for the year of sale, and partnerships/S corporations make the election at the partner/shareholder level. The amendment applies to sales or exchanges in taxable years beginning after the date of enactment.

**Sec. 70438.** This section extends the rules for treatment of certain disaster-related personal casualty losses by substituting the date of enactment of this section for the date of enactment of the Taxpayer Certainty and Disaster Tax Relief Act of 2020 in Section 304(b) of that Act. This effectively extends the period during which certain disaster losses are deductible.

**Sec. 70439.** This section restores the taxable REIT subsidiary asset test by increasing the limit from 20% to 25% of the REIT's total assets. This allows REITs to hold a larger portion of their assets in taxable subsidiaries without losing their REIT status. The amendment applies to taxable years beginning after December 31, 2025. [end of text]


